WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.608 --> 00:00:09.609
My name is Joe Gall.

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:12.445
I'm at the Carnegie Institution in Baltimore

00:00:12.446 --> 00:00:14.514
in the Department of Embryology,

00:00:14.515 --> 00:00:16.516
and I'm going to talk today

00:00:16.517 --> 00:00:19.218
about in situ hybridization.

00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:22.555
In situ hybridization is the technique

00:00:22.556 --> 00:00:25.892
by which a labeled, single-stranded

00:00:25.893 --> 00:00:29.328
RNA or DNA molecule in solution

00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:32.431
is hybridized to immobilized

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:35.635
single-stranded RNA or DNA in a tissue

00:00:35.636 --> 00:00:37.837
or tissue section.

00:00:37.838 --> 00:00:43.709
And by having a tag on the molecule in solution,

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.712
eventually the hybrid is visible

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:48.414
in the tissue section

00:00:48.415 --> 00:00:50.516
as viewed through a microscope.

00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:52.385
This technique of in situ hybridization

00:00:52.386 --> 00:00:54.287
was first carried out successfully

00:00:54.288 --> 00:00:57.290
in my laboratory in 1968.

00:00:57.291 --> 00:01:00.026
The origin of in situ hybridization,

00:01:00.027 --> 00:01:03.996
at least as an intellectual phenomenon,

00:01:03.997 --> 00:01:05.965
derives from experiments

00:01:05.966 --> 00:01:09.268
that had been carried out much earlier,

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:11.704
mainly around 1950,

00:01:11.705 --> 00:01:14.874
by two investigators named Coons and Kaplan,

00:01:14.875 --> 00:01:17.743
and what Coons and Kaplan had done

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:22.248
was to develop the technique of immunocytochemistry,

00:01:22.249 --> 00:01:25.117
a technique by which

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:28.621
a fluorescent antibody

00:01:28.622 --> 00:01:31.791
was made to bind to the antigen

00:01:31.792 --> 00:01:34.327
immobilized in a tissue section,

00:01:34.328 --> 00:01:38.030
so that one could detect the position of

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:41.634
specific protein molecules in cells

00:01:41.635 --> 00:01:43.569
by their fluorescence.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.539
And shown here is a modern image.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:52.144
This is a nuclear body from a salamander oocyte.

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:55.014
Here, shows immunostaining

00:01:55.015 --> 00:02:00.152
with an antibody against the protein Coilin.

00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:02.154
Here's immunostaining with an antibody

00:02:02.155 --> 00:02:04.557
against an Sm protein.

00:02:04.558 --> 00:02:07.827
This is a slide which I made more recently,

00:02:07.828 --> 00:02:11.597
using the Coons and Kaplan technique.

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:14.634
The point, however, is that

00:02:14.635 --> 00:02:17.069
they had shown that it was possible to detect

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.539
specific protein molecules in tissues,

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.641
and it occurred to me that it would be wonderful

00:02:21.642 --> 00:02:26.679
if one could detect specific nucleic acids in tissues.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.448
Actually, I was thinking less about nucleic acids

00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:31.784
as I was thinking about genes,

00:02:31.785 --> 00:02:34.086
because I had been studying the giant chromosomes

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:36.622
of salamanders, shown here,

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:38.891
or the giant chromosomes of Drosophila,

00:02:38.892 --> 00:02:40.526
shown here,

00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:42.762
and in these chromosomes

00:02:42.763 --> 00:02:45.498
one can see bands

00:02:45.499 --> 00:02:48.768
that were pretty much regarded as genes

00:02:48.769 --> 00:02:50.970
by people working with them at that time.

00:02:50.971 --> 00:02:53.539
We now know this is a little bit more complicated than that,

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:58.544
but it seemed that we had here a tissue

00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:00.813
- a chromosome -

00:03:00.814 --> 00:03:02.915
where specific genes

00:03:02.916 --> 00:03:05.585
- specific nucleic acids...

00:03:05.586 --> 00:03:08.688
it should be possible to do something comparable to the

00:03:08.689 --> 00:03:11.591
immunofluorescent staining technique

00:03:11.592 --> 00:03:15.595
to detect specific genes in these tissues.

00:03:15.596 --> 00:03:19.765
So, actually,

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:23.870
I kept this in my mind for quite a long time,

00:03:23.871 --> 00:03:27.974
and it became possible to think about doing this kind of thing

00:03:27.975 --> 00:03:30.176
in the early 1960s,

00:03:30.177 --> 00:03:33.679
when investigators discovered that

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.116
one could take double-stranded DNA,

00:03:37.117 --> 00:03:40.286
denature it into its single strands,

00:03:40.287 --> 00:03:43.022
and immobilize those strands

00:03:43.023 --> 00:03:46.926
on a nitrocellulose filter.

00:03:46.927 --> 00:03:50.329
This was done by a couple of investigators

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.465
named Gillespie and Spiegelman,

00:03:52.466 --> 00:03:54.600
and what they showed was that,

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:57.603
having the denatured DNA on a filter,

00:03:57.604 --> 00:04:00.306
one could then hybridize

00:04:00.307 --> 00:04:02.842
radioactive RNA to it

00:04:02.843 --> 00:04:05.811
and actually determine, quantitatively,

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:09.482
the amount of a particular gene sequence on the filter.

00:04:09.483 --> 00:04:11.951
So, I thought,

00:04:11.952 --> 00:04:13.686
"Well, if they can do it on a filter

00:04:13.687 --> 00:04:15.821
you ought to be able to do it on a slide".

00:04:15.822 --> 00:04:21.861
So, I tried denaturing the DNA on a slide,

00:04:21.862 --> 00:04:25.498
that is, in cells - nuclei -

00:04:25.499 --> 00:04:28.201
that were squashed out onto a slide,

00:04:28.202 --> 00:04:31.704
and I found that I could denature the DNA quite adequately,

00:04:31.705 --> 00:04:34.073
without damaging the tissue,

00:04:34.074 --> 00:04:36.342
but then when I tried to hybridize

00:04:36.343 --> 00:04:38.778
a radioactive RNA to it,

00:04:38.779 --> 00:04:40.813
I got no signal.

00:04:40.814 --> 00:04:42.648
In this case,

00:04:42.649 --> 00:04:46.219
I was looking for the radioactivity by autoradiography,

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:48.654
that is by covering the tissue

00:04:48.655 --> 00:04:51.824
with a thin layer of photographic emulsion

00:04:51.825 --> 00:04:55.194
and looking for silver grains in the emulsion

00:04:55.195 --> 00:04:59.098
caused by the radioactivity of the probe.

00:04:59.099 --> 00:05:01.300
As I said, nothing happened,

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:05.271
and I more or less gave up on that technique.

00:05:05.272 --> 00:05:09.008
That was in the early to mid 1960s.

00:05:09.009 --> 00:05:14.514
Now, a break came, quite accidentally,

00:05:14.515 --> 00:05:16.782
from studies that I and others

00:05:16.783 --> 00:05:20.119
had been carrying out

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:22.889
on salamander oocytes and frog oocytes,

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:27.426
and what we found was that

00:05:27.427 --> 00:05:29.962
in the giant oocyte nucleus,

00:05:29.963 --> 00:05:32.532
which is shown here,

00:05:32.533 --> 00:05:36.536
derived from this large oocyte here

00:05:36.537 --> 00:05:39.539
- the oocyte is the egg of the frog -

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.575
inside of this giant nucleus

00:05:42.576 --> 00:05:44.610
are of course the chromosomes,

00:05:44.611 --> 00:05:48.014
but in addition we found that

00:05:48.015 --> 00:05:51.184
there were several hundred to a thousand nucleoli,

00:05:51.185 --> 00:05:55.621
these round bodies here shown on this particular slide.

00:05:55.622 --> 00:06:01.093
Now, the interesting thing about these nucleoli

00:06:01.094 --> 00:06:03.796
that we discovered was that

00:06:03.797 --> 00:06:06.132
they contained DNA,

00:06:06.133 --> 00:06:08.601
and it was specifically the genes

00:06:08.602 --> 00:06:11.003
that code for ribosomal RNA

00:06:11.004 --> 00:06:13.539
that were in these nucleoli.

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:15.141
This was a most unusual situation

00:06:15.142 --> 00:06:17.577
to have DNA outside of the chromosomes,

00:06:17.578 --> 00:06:19.679
but it was quite clear from work

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.681
that had been carried out in my laboratory,

00:06:21.682 --> 00:06:23.683
as well as in the laboratory of Don Brown

00:06:23.684 --> 00:06:26.085
and of Oscar Miller,

00:06:26.086 --> 00:06:28.988
that there was extrachromosomal DNA

00:06:28.989 --> 00:06:32.391
outside in these nucleoli.

00:06:32.392 --> 00:06:35.528
And I studied the origin of that DNA

00:06:35.529 --> 00:06:38.998
and what I found was that

00:06:38.999 --> 00:06:42.034
it actually was synthesized at one specific time

00:06:42.035 --> 00:06:47.907
in the early history of the formation of the oocyte,

00:06:47.908 --> 00:06:52.778
and one could see in these early cells

00:06:52.779 --> 00:06:54.780
- early nuclei -

00:06:54.781 --> 00:06:58.017
that there was a cap of DNA here...

00:06:58.018 --> 00:07:00.720
and you can see the cap over here,

00:07:00.721 --> 00:07:03.022
you can see the chromosomes here

00:07:03.023 --> 00:07:06.759
and down here you can see the chromosomes in this nucleus...

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:08.728
so, in these two nuclei

00:07:08.729 --> 00:07:10.530
you have the chromosomes,

00:07:10.531 --> 00:07:13.733
but then you have this huge cap of

00:07:13.734 --> 00:07:17.136
extra DNA which in fact

00:07:17.137 --> 00:07:20.306
was the ribosomal genes

00:07:20.307 --> 00:07:22.508
that had been somehow...

00:07:22.509 --> 00:07:24.243
had somehow come out of the chromosome

00:07:24.244 --> 00:07:26.913
and amplified to give you

00:07:26.914 --> 00:07:28.915
this one specific sequence,

00:07:28.916 --> 00:07:31.884
a very large amount of this one specific sequence.

00:07:31.885 --> 00:07:34.053
So, it occurred to me that if there was any possibility

00:07:34.054 --> 00:07:36.222
of doing in situ hybridization,

00:07:36.223 --> 00:07:38.624
this was the case that would work.

00:07:38.625 --> 00:07:42.595
And so I took a radioactive ribosomal RNA

00:07:42.596 --> 00:07:47.066
and hybridized it to cells of this sort,

00:07:47.067 --> 00:07:49.435
and I was quite pleased

00:07:49.436 --> 00:07:52.205
to see in the very first experiments

00:07:52.206 --> 00:07:54.540
that we got good in situ hybridization.

00:07:54.541 --> 00:07:56.776
Here, this cell is black

00:07:56.777 --> 00:08:00.112
because of the silver grains in the photographic emulsion

00:08:00.113 --> 00:08:03.149
that had been exposed by the radioactivity

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:05.117
of the hybridizing probe.

00:08:05.118 --> 00:08:10.223
So, this is a case where we have hybridized

00:08:10.224 --> 00:08:13.359
radioactive RNA - ribosomal RNA -

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:18.764
to the ribosomal DNA in the cell.

00:08:18.765 --> 00:08:21.167
So, that led immediately to a publication

00:08:21.168 --> 00:08:23.669
with my student Mary Lou Pardue,

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:27.507
who joined me in this work,

00:08:27.508 --> 00:08:31.444
and this was the first successful in situ hybridization.

00:08:31.445 --> 00:08:33.579
Now, we wanted to try it

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:36.182
on some other tissue or DNA,

00:08:36.183 --> 00:08:38.684
but there were very few examples,

00:08:38.685 --> 00:08:41.821
because at that time it was not possible

00:08:41.822 --> 00:08:47.760
to isolate specific DNA or RNA sequences.

00:08:47.761 --> 00:08:51.664
What permitted us to look at another situation

00:08:51.665 --> 00:08:55.034
came from studies that had been carried out

00:08:55.035 --> 00:08:58.004
on mouse DNA,

00:08:58.005 --> 00:09:00.840
and investigators had found that

00:09:00.841 --> 00:09:03.176
when mouse DNA was spun

00:09:03.177 --> 00:09:04.610
in a cesium chloride (CsCl) gradient,

00:09:04.611 --> 00:09:06.512
it separated into two fractions,

00:09:06.513 --> 00:09:09.815
a main peak and a satellite peak

00:09:09.816 --> 00:09:12.418
- called satellite simply because

00:09:12.419 --> 00:09:15.121
it had a slightly different density

00:09:15.122 --> 00:09:18.824
and separated from the bulk of the DNA.

00:09:18.825 --> 00:09:21.661
We took this satellite DNA,

00:09:21.662 --> 00:09:23.796
made it radioactive,

00:09:23.797 --> 00:09:26.599
and we hybridized it to mouse chromosomes,

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:30.002
not knowing at all whether it would hybridize,

00:09:30.003 --> 00:09:32.505
whether it was even in the chromosomes for that matter,

00:09:32.506 --> 00:09:35.508
it might have been a virus or some other infective particle,

00:09:35.509 --> 00:09:39.212
but we were very pleased to see that

00:09:39.213 --> 00:09:41.714
the mouse satellite DNA hybridized

00:09:41.715 --> 00:09:45.585
specifically to one portion of each chromosome,

00:09:45.586 --> 00:09:48.454
and you can see on this slide

00:09:48.455 --> 00:09:51.090
that each of the chromosomes

00:09:51.091 --> 00:09:53.593
has a bit of radioactivity

00:09:53.594 --> 00:09:55.728
at the very end of the chromosome.

00:09:55.729 --> 00:09:57.763
This region of the chromosome

00:09:57.764 --> 00:09:59.966
was known as the heterochromatin,

00:09:59.967 --> 00:10:02.702
and had been known cytologically

00:10:02.703 --> 00:10:05.805
as a specific region of the chromosome

00:10:05.806 --> 00:10:08.174
for a number of years.

00:10:08.175 --> 00:10:10.910
This led us immediately to think

00:10:10.911 --> 00:10:14.247
that we should be able to try the same technique

00:10:14.248 --> 00:10:16.582
and get similar results

00:10:16.583 --> 00:10:19.151
looking at the chromosomes of Drosophila,

00:10:19.152 --> 00:10:21.487
and the chromosomes of Drosophila

00:10:21.488 --> 00:10:23.456
were especially exciting

00:10:23.457 --> 00:10:26.359
because it was known from genetics

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:32.532
that the genes were all in

00:10:32.533 --> 00:10:35.768
the so-called euchromatic regions of the chromosome,

00:10:35.769 --> 00:10:42.108
namely this region from here to here, or over here...

00:10:42.109 --> 00:10:44.143
these lighter stained regions...

00:10:44.144 --> 00:10:46.078
all of the genes were located in those regions,

00:10:46.079 --> 00:10:48.314
and it was known from genetics

00:10:48.315 --> 00:10:52.585
that the heterochromatic regions, like here and here,

00:10:52.586 --> 00:10:55.188
were devoid of genes.

00:10:55.189 --> 00:10:58.257
So, it occurred to us that Drosophila

00:10:58.258 --> 00:11:01.160
must have a lot of DNA

00:11:01.161 --> 00:11:04.430
similar to the satellite DNA of the mouse.

00:11:04.431 --> 00:11:07.834
So, we centrifuged that DNA

00:11:07.835 --> 00:11:10.269
in a cesium chloride gradient,

00:11:10.270 --> 00:11:12.505
and we found, indeed,

00:11:12.506 --> 00:11:15.641
that Drosophila had a lot of satellite DNA.

00:11:15.642 --> 00:11:17.877
This species of Drosophila virilis

00:11:17.878 --> 00:11:20.613
that we were working on

00:11:20.614 --> 00:11:22.882
had three huge satellite peaks

00:11:22.883 --> 00:11:26.185
- here, here, and here -

00:11:26.186 --> 00:11:31.824
in addition to the main peak of DNA, shown here.

00:11:31.825 --> 00:11:34.594
So, we took the satellite DNA,

00:11:34.595 --> 00:11:36.596
we made it radioactive,

00:11:36.597 --> 00:11:38.331
we hybridized it to chromosomes,

00:11:38.332 --> 00:11:41.334
and, sure enough, it hybridized as shown

00:11:41.335 --> 00:11:42.735
on these images here,

00:11:42.736 --> 00:11:44.804
to the heterochromatic regions.

00:11:44.805 --> 00:11:46.906
And this was particular interesting,

00:11:46.907 --> 00:11:48.808
as I said,

00:11:48.809 --> 00:11:51.677
because it showed that the heterochromatic regions,

00:11:51.678 --> 00:11:54.180
that we knew from genetics had no genes,

00:11:54.181 --> 00:11:58.451
consisted of this satellite-type DNA,

00:11:58.452 --> 00:12:01.254
which our later studies showed

00:12:01.255 --> 00:12:03.956
consisted of very simple sequences,

00:12:03.957 --> 00:12:08.661
which were incapable of coding for genes,

00:12:08.662 --> 00:12:12.298
or were incapable of being genes and coding for proteins.

00:12:12.299 --> 00:12:14.634
So, the in situ hybridization technique

00:12:14.635 --> 00:12:17.036
at this point was limited

00:12:17.037 --> 00:12:19.539
pretty much to sequences

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:22.341
that were present in large amount in cells.

00:12:22.342 --> 00:12:25.444
Eventually, it became possible,

00:12:25.445 --> 00:12:28.314
by having more and more sensitive probes,

00:12:28.315 --> 00:12:32.485
in particular the later techniques were worked out

00:12:32.486 --> 00:12:34.887
for making fluorescent probes

00:12:34.888 --> 00:12:37.557
to do in situ hybridization...

00:12:37.558 --> 00:12:39.325
so, in situ hybridization,

00:12:39.326 --> 00:12:41.060
ISH or "ish",

00:12:41.061 --> 00:12:43.296
became fluorescent in situ hybridization,

00:12:43.297 --> 00:12:46.032
or "fish", FISH,

00:12:46.033 --> 00:12:49.502
which is the was it's usually practiced now.

00:12:49.503 --> 00:12:51.838
And techniques have gotten

00:12:51.839 --> 00:12:53.673
more and more sensitive,

00:12:53.674 --> 00:12:55.208
to the point where, nowadays,

00:12:55.209 --> 00:12:57.510
it's possible to detect individual,

00:12:57.511 --> 00:13:24.671
single DNA and RNA molecules in tissue sections.

